---
import SeoArticle from "../../components/SeoArticle.astro";
import { faq } from "./faq";
---

<SeoArticle idPrefix="la-seo" {faq}>
	<section aria-labelledby="la-seo-how">
		<h2 id="la-seo-how" class="sd-text-title">How to use the ladder</h2>
		<ol>
			<li>Write one line from your piece. It can be a small detail or a big idea.</li>
			<li>
				Press So what? to climb. Write what the line below means, or what it's an example of.
			</li>
			<li>
				Press Like what? to step down. Write something a reader could see, hear, or touch that shows the line
				above.
			</li>
			<li>
				Read your rungs as a passage. Strike the middle rungs and see how a high line reads right next to a low
				one.
			</li>
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="la-seo-why">
		<h2 id="la-seo-why" class="sd-text-title">Why move up and down</h2>
		<p>
			A reader needs both ends. Details alone are a pile of objects with no reason to care about them. Ideas
			alone are claims with nothing to picture. Most pieces that land move between the two: a mug, then what the
			mug means, then the next thing she does with it.
		</p>
		<p>
			Hayakawa called it "dead-level abstracting" when a writer stays on one rung for too long. Climbing the
			ladder line by line is a quick way to notice where you've been standing, and to find the rung you're
			missing.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="la-seo-history">
		<h2 id="la-seo-history" class="sd-text-title">Where the ladder comes from</h2>
		<p>
			The abstraction ladder is S. I. Hayakawa's. He introduced it in <cite>Language in Action</cite> and carried
			it into <cite>Language in Thought and Action</cite> (1949), a book about how words shape what we think we
			know. Hayakawa adapted the idea from Alfred Korzybski's structural differential, a teaching diagram from
			general semantics. Since then, journalism and writing teachers have adapted it for the page, among them Jack
			Hart in <cite>Storycraft</cite>. This tool is our own take on the idea; if it's useful to you, Hayakawa's
			book is well worth reading.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="la-seo-privacy">
		<h2 id="la-seo-privacy" class="sd-text-title">Your ladder is yours</h2>
		<p>
			Nothing you write here leaves your computer. Your ladder is saved in your browser so it's waiting when you
			come back. There is no account.
		</p>
	</section>
</SeoArticle>
